{"title":"1998 AIME Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_-_Unrigorous_engineers_induction_solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 - Unrigorous engineers induction solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_-_Counting_Head_on\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 - Counting Head on</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the number of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ordered_pair\" title=\"Ordered pair\">ordered pairs</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> of positive integers that satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/9491c9233a3d4f6eab4190e6584b5200c070092d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\le 2y \\le 60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/7/cb7cf74aac510a2ff3680bf088b6340ad62196ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\le 2x \\le 60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AIME_1998-2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2a/AIME_1998-2.png/300px-AIME_1998-2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"283\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/2a/AIME_1998-2.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pick%27s_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Pick&#39;s theorem\">Pick's theorem</a> states that:\n</p>\n<div style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/b/7cba92d5eeba4957d9f6127c3a7243cf3cbe15ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = I + \\frac B2 - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"118\" height=\"37\" /></div>\n<p>The conditions give us four <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inequality\" title=\"Inequality\">inequalities</a>: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/e/48e9b3808fa6ec45b3f975913bd31b93c8bfc3ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\le 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/7/707abae8cd9616b5e6c2c90915b962b0607a087f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y\\le 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/d/7bdc214696f95cc70e2d588fc00aaa0b42b52a06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\le 2y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/8/e08b21d4edaf6713422481ef65f0ecd5ed9f9275.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\le 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" />. These create a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Quadrilateral\" title=\"Quadrilateral\">quadrilateral</a>, whose area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/c/3dcd5e6a7667f87063bc60523fe5945cd667a2be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the 30 by 30 <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square\" title=\"Square\">square</a> it is in. A simple way to see this is to note that the two triangles outside of the quadrilateral form half of the area of the 30 by 30 square.\n</p><p>So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dc7cd89e1d9827ae34d366507d79d517d2a0e6d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\frac 12 \\cdot 30^2 = 450$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"140\" height=\"37\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> we can calculate by just counting. Ignoring the vertices, the top and right sides have 14 <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Lattice_point\" title=\"Lattice point\">lattice points</a>, and the two diagonals each have 14 lattice points (for the top diagonal, every value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> corresponds with an integer value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/d/e8d3f41e4ce3512f73fcda8ef2c9c236f7143a41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" /> and vice versa for the bottom, so and there are 14 values for x not counting vertices). Adding the four vertices, there are 60 points on the borders.\n</p>\n<div style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/b/ebb7fcc51788e8575f7924fc60da042ab8f4ade4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$450 = I + \\frac {60}2 - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"135\" height=\"37\" /><br /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/8/e385581c8c77f9dcb0b15e14772efc79d72970e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I = 421$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /></div>\n<p>Since the inequalities also include the equals case, we include the boundaries, which gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/3/5b33532c6ace08dc49b933eccc0a4a9aef65ed8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$421 + 60 = 481$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"118\" height=\"14\" /> ordered pairs. However, the question asks us for positive integers, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> doesn't count; hence, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/1/5019ae5ab5a63c85111154e82ebcc78eedcdbe86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$480$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>First, note that all pairs of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb3c8b44f6786741864d61e9e40c3bf9c81ac8e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/5/7259e6ffee7bec38a27f5b9aa36bc53fc34e23c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le a\\le30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"84\" height=\"15\" /> work.\n</p><p>Now, considered the ordered pairs with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/4/f94c45489bc9bbeae5ab98827205179e938f7c76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa8399d5a12e28781468fb19acf70895aa668364.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 2y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" /> is automatically satisfied. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/c/e9c6b003522d4173841abd19bc6fd76eabebf32a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; y\\le 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"15\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/2/c72493fb382310c364404c1e99f8aecda88f04a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x - x = x$\" width=\"86\" height=\"12\" /> possible values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. Hence, given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> values of possible <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/4/f94c45489bc9bbeae5ab98827205179e938f7c76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> and the above conditions are satisfied. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/5/4252d917f416754f35e021cd0bae20ce64dccfb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y\\le60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" />, so this only works for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c68992b5529943fc2dab56d5a74b4fd70370c81c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\le15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, there are\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05e037917a2c45002b202b85620717a056765b20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=1}^{15} i=\\frac{(15)(16)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"126\" height=\"50\" />\n</p><p>ordered pairs. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/4/ab474507be3f0defa788b9a77282269becb2cb20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> must follow <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb68bb2dd11b3bdf4b90e25bb5b2c3d429ddf89e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; y\\le 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"86\" height=\"16\" />. Hence, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/2/1d2351a9ff42ede4103dfc074ebca94aded29d20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30 - x$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> possibilities for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and there are\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5ada07792ee89dadf2f169b5c565114de06598d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=16}^{30}(30-i)=\\sum_{i=0}^{14}i=\\frac{(14)(15)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"239\" height=\"50\" />\n</p><p>ordered pairs.\n</p><p>By symmetry, there are also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/f/e3f09e1d7c9f875caf994bc8381bbc96793e71d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {(15)(16)}{2} + \\frac {(14)(15)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"158\" height=\"38\" /> ordered pairs with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab7da572d60053bdb3ab92f2dda44f87cd9dc78d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> and the above criteria satisfied.\n</p><p>Hence, the total is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/6/0f6c07aac52c955012d60d235cecf63b81e9aa6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(15)(16)}{2}+\\frac{(14)(15)}{2}+\\frac{(15)(16)}{2}+\\frac{(14)(15)}{2}+30=480.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"436\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/7/1b78922cc42a69ce128080f1f86b317b238135b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y\\le2x\\le60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Multiplying both sides by 2 yields:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1ec0e01293947e9c5df02b722483e02decc82491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y\\le4x\\le120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Then the two inequalities can be merged to form the following inequality:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/0/6d0718e2eca4e9361807690c3717b294c8224ad2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\le2y\\le4x\\le120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"148\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Additionally, we must ensure that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/f/1ef0ee95ef67809f4c072e1a5abd0abca1ccb9cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y&lt;60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Therefore we must find pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> that satisfy the inequality above. \nA bit of trial and error and observing patterns leads to the answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/1/5019ae5ab5a63c85111154e82ebcc78eedcdbe86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$480$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>It should be noted that the cases for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c68992b5529943fc2dab56d5a74b4fd70370c81c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\le15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/6/7f68bb5b854655564223842bd034ae4723b10ec8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;15$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> should be considered separately in order to ensure that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/9/5c98dfddc2fe6263fa86c8837a3f21b621080500.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y &lt; 60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_-_Unrigorous_engineers_induction_solution\">Solution 4 - Unrigorous engineers induction solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 - Unrigorous engineers induction solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We will try out small cases.\n</p><p>By replacing 60 in this problem with 2, we count only 1 ordered pair. By doing with 4, we count 4 ordered pairs. With 6, we get 7 pairs. With 8 we get 12. By continuing on, and then finding the difference between adjacent terms (1,3,3,5,5,...). We suspect that if 60 was replaced with 2n, we will find 1+3+3+5+5+7+7 ...., where there will be n terms. Thus, our answer is 1+3+3+5+5.... 29+29+31 = 16*30 = 480.\n</p><p>-Alexlikemath\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_-_Counting_Head_on\">Solution 5 - Counting Head on</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 - Counting Head on\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Notice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> both must be equal or less than 30. The inequalities given have no complicated qualities. We can recompile them by understanding: Two times the larger integer will also be larger than the smaller integer; Two times the smaller integer is greater or equal to the greater integer if and only if the greater integer is less or equal to the double of the smaller integer. Knowing this, we create a chart. We will first solve without order, then multiply pairs by 2 at the end. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> can be 1-30, so we'll start with 1. The only possible value for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is 1. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/f/2effc7979b1c93e59356b73253d75e2740b33105.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> can be 2-4. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/0/690ef55a607beaf2389d1ee102df817721bc2392.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> can be 3-6. There is an obvious pattern here. For every integer after 1, the possible values for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> will be numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />. This predictably ends at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/a/89a4662a1dcd96b0a6bc7d5abfe65f22109532a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> will reach 30. When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/c/d2cae13a8f6458d5d8c61ba965d0abb4b08da072.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />, then the number of possible values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> will begin to drop again, equaling the amount when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/7559c5589729b2783559c301b88989b3b24e17a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />. Then when we finally sum the group together, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/f/2effc7979b1c93e59356b73253d75e2740b33105.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/7559c5589729b2783559c301b88989b3b24e17a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> there are 104 pairs with 2 distinct values, and 13 values with congruent values. These will not be multiplied by 2 later on. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/c/d2cae13a8f6458d5d8c61ba965d0abb4b08da072.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/3/b73c64f6b2d79cee4ae6ca58e30a71557dcc9611.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 28$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> gives the same amount. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/a/89a4662a1dcd96b0a6bc7d5abfe65f22109532a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/a/16a9e6c98137ccce8a57cf29a46e210d6e9d403e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 29$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> gives 16 and 2 values respectively, with two congruent values each. Finally, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/34310f2f36ed6d724838edb08788ee62acb33386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/c/d3cbc01b2d2b1cd07002c305521aaf884a3b43de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 30$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> give 3 and 1 respectively.\n</p><p>Sum them together and you will get 480.\n</p><p><br />\n-jackshi2006\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_AIME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<t